I can help — first, a couple quick clarifying points so my suggestions are accurate:

- Are you male or female? (osteopenia screening and some labs differ by sex)
- Did you mean your last A1C was 7.2% or 12%? A1C 1.2% is almost certainly a typo.
- What is the actual GERD medication? (You listed metoprolol twice — metoprolol isn’t used for GERD.)
- Do you know why you’re on apixaban (e.g., atrial fibrillation, prior DVT/PE, mechanical valve)? That affects cardiology follow-up.

Below I’ll give (A) focused questions to bring to your annual physical, (B) tests you can ask your PCP to order, (C) how osteopenia is usually managed (including when to start medication), (D) special considerations given your meds and diabetes/retinopathy, and (E) when a cardiology referral is appropriate. At the end I can draft a short message to Dr. Wilson if you want.

A — Questions to ask at the visit
- For bone health
  - Do I need a DEXA scan now given my age, diabetes, and history of retinopathy?
  - What is my FRAX 10‑year fracture risk (please calculate with my DEXA if you have it)?
  - What labs should we run to look for secondary causes of low bone density?
  - If I have osteopenia, what non‑drug measures should I start and do I meet criteria for medication?
  - If medication is recommended, what are the options (oral bisphosphonate vs IV vs denosumab) and which is best with my other meds/conditions?
- For cardiology/cardiac risk
  - Given my age, diabetes, and current meds (apixaban, metoprolol), do you recommend routine cardiology follow-up at Colorado Cardiology or can you manage this?
  - Are there any additional cardiac tests you recommend before deciding on a cardiology referral (ECG, echocardiogram, lipid panel, A1C review)?
  - Do my anticoagulation and blood pressure medicines need specialist review?

B — Tests and evaluations to request (useful for deciding management)
- DEXA scan of lumbar spine and hip (if none in the last 2–3 years)
- FRAX calculation (ideally using femoral neck BMD from DEXA)
- Labs: serum 25‑hydroxyvitamin D, calcium, creatinine/eGFR, CMP, TSH, CBC. Consider testosterone (men) and celiac serology if suggestive history. If unexpected lab findings, additional workup (e.g., SPEP) may be considered.
- Fall-risk assessment (gait/balance, home hazards)
- Baseline ECG — useful if cardiology referral is being considered
- Lipid panel and repeat A1C (confirm the correct value)

C — How osteopenia/osteoporosis is usually handled (practical summary)
- Who to screen: Routine DEXA is recommended earlier than age-based thresholds when you have major risk factors (long-standing diabetes, low body weight, use of medications that affect bone, prior fragility fracture, etc.). Diabetes increases fracture risk even when BMD isn’t profoundly low.
- When to treat: Generally, treat with pharmacologic therapy if:
  - T-score ≤ −2.5 (osteoporosis), OR
  - T-score between −1.0 and −2.5 (osteopenia) PLUS FRAX 10‑year hip fracture risk ≥3% or major osteoporotic fracture ≥20% (thresholds used commonly in the U.S.), OR
  - Prior fragility fracture (hip or spine) or very high fracture risk.
- First-line meds: Oral bisphosphonates (alendronate weekly or risedronate) are typical first-line; yearly IV zoledronic acid is an alternative if GI intolerance or adherence concern. Denosumab (subQ q6 months) is an option for some patients (note rebound fracture risk if stopped without follow-up). Anabolic agents (teriparatide, abaloparatide, romosozumab) are for very high-risk patients.
- Non‑drug measures: adequate calcium (diet ± supplement to reach ~1,000–1,200 mg/day), vitamin D supplementation to maintain 25‑OH vitamin D >30 ng/mL (typical doses 800–2,000 IU/day depending on level), weight‑bearing and resistance exercises, smoking cessation, limit alcohol, fall prevention/balance training, review home hazards and other meds that increase fall risk.

D — Special considerations with your conditions/meds
- Type 2 diabetes: diabetes increases fracture risk and may worsen bone quality despite normal BMD. This lowers the threshold for screening/treatment consideration.
- Retinopathy: visual impairment increases fall risk — strengthens the case to screen and to address fall prevention.
- Apixaban (anticoagulant): there is no major drug interaction that prevents starting oral bisphosphonates. Injection site bleeding with denosumab is usually minimal; IV zoledronic acid infusions are done while anticoagulated in many cases but you may need coordination if an invasive procedure is planned. If a fracture requires surgery or vertebral procedures, anticoagulation management is important — discuss perioperative plans with the team.
- GERD and bisphosphonates: oral bisphosphonates can irritate the esophagus. If you have active GERD, options include using a weekly alendronate with precautions, switching to an alternative formulation (risedronate delayed-release), or using IV zoledronic acid or denosumab instead.
- Kidney function: bisphosphonates should be used cautiously if eGFR <30–35 mL/min; denosumab can be used in renal impairment but monitor calcium.

E — When to refer to cardiology (Colorado Cardiology)
Recommend cardiology referral if any of the following apply:
- You have atrial fibrillation, recent stroke/TIA, prior DVT/PE, or the apixaban was started by cardiology and needs ongoing specialist management.
- New or worsening cardiac symptoms: chest pain, syncope/pre‑syncope, progressive dyspnea on exertion, new palpitations, or signs of heart failure.
- Complex anticoagulation decisions (major bleeding while on apixaban, planned procedures that require anticoagulation interruption, uncertain indication).
- Uncontrolled arrhythmia or if PCP asks for cardiology to help optimize rate/rhythm control (metoprolol dosing) or consider ablation/advanced therapies.
- New murmur, abnormal echocardiogram, or testing suggests structural heart disease.

If none of the above applies and your anticoagulation/symptoms have been stable, many PCPs will co-manage with periodic cardiology input as needed. Still, given you’re on apixaban and metoprolol, it’s reasonable to ask Dr. Wilson whether he wants to continue managing these or refer to Colorado Cardiology for ongoing specialty follow-up.

Suggested wording to bring to the visit or send to Dr. Wilson
(If you’d like, I can draft a short email for you to send.)
- “I’d like to review bone health at my annual physical. Given my type 2 diabetes and history of retinopathy, should I get a DEXA scan and FRAX assessment now? Please order DEXA and the labs you think are appropriate (vitamin D, calcium, CMP, TSH). If I have osteopenia, what treatment threshold do you recommend? Also, given I’m on apixaban and metoprolol, do you recommend a cardiology referral to Colorado Cardiology for ongoing anticoagulation/arrhythmia care or can you continue to manage?”

Would you like me to:
- Draft a short email/message to Dr. Wilson requesting DEXA and the labs and asking about cardiology referral? OR
- Draft a concise list you can print and bring to your appointment?

And please confirm your sex and what the correct A1C and GERD med are so I can tailor recommendations precisely.

