I can help — this is a good set of things to prepare. A few quick clarifying questions before I go deeper (these will change recommendations):
- Is the A1C really 1.2, or was that a typo (e.g., 6.2 or 7.2)?  
- Why are you on apixaban (atrial fibrillation, prior DVT/PE, other)?  
- Are you already followed by cardiology for the apixaban/metoprolol, or only by your PCP?  
- What sex are you, and have you had a DEXA scan before (and any history of fractures)?  
If you prefer, I can draft a short message to Dr. Wilson asking these questions and requesting particular tests — say so and I’ll draft it.

Below are practical points you can use at your annual visit, organized into (A) osteopenia screening/management and (B) whether/when to get a cardiology referral — plus a concise list of questions to ask your PCP.

A. Osteopenia — what to consider and ask
1. Why this matters for you
- Type 2 diabetes increases fracture risk even when bone density looks normal.  
- Retinopathy increases fall risk (vision impairment → more falls).  
- Age (52) and any other risk factors (prior fracture, family history of hip fracture, low BMI, chronic steroid use, smoking, high alcohol intake) change when to screen or treat.

2. Tests to consider/request
- DEXA scan (bone mineral density) — if you haven’t had one in the past 2–5 years, ask for it now. Men age 50–69 and women <65 with risk factors (like diabetes) are reasonable candidates.  
- Ask provider to calculate your 10-year fracture risk with FRAX (this uses DEXA T-score plus clinical risk factors). Typical treatment thresholds used in the U.S.: treat if 10‑yr hip fracture risk ≥3% or major osteoporotic fracture risk ≥20%, or if T-score ≤ −2.5 or prior fragility fracture.  
- Labs: serum 25‑hydroxyvitamin D, calcium, creatinine (kidney function), and sometimes TSH and vitamin D if indicated. These guide supplementation and medication choices.

3. Lifestyle and supplements to discuss
- Calcium intake (dietary first; supplement only if needed) and vitamin D supplementation guided by level. Typical vitamin D target is 25‑OH D >30 ng/mL (provider-specific).  
- Weight‑bearing and resistance exercise, balance training, home safety to reduce fall risk.  
- Smoking cessation and limiting heavy alcohol.  
- Review meds that affect bone/falls (ask whether any of your meds—PPI use, some SSRIs, long-term steroids—are relevant).

4. When to consider medication (bisphosphonates and others)
- If DEXA shows osteoporosis (T-score ≤ −2.5) → treatment usually recommended.  
- If osteopenia (T-score between −1.0 and −2.5), treat if FRAX risk above thresholds (hip ≥3% or major ≥20%) or if prior fragility fracture.  
- First‑line: oral bisphosphonate (alendronate) or IV zoledronic acid depending on GI tolerance, kidney function, and adherence. Alternatives (denosumab, teriparatide) used for specific situations.  
- Discuss duration and monitoring (e.g., repeat DEXA in 1–3 years depending on results/treatment).

5. Questions to bring to your PCP about bone health
- “Have I had a DEXA? If not, can we schedule one? If yes, when was it and what did it show?”  
- “Can you calculate my FRAX score and tell me if I meet treatment thresholds?”  
- “Do I need vitamin D or calcium testing/supplementation?”  
- “Given my diabetes and retinopathy, should I start pharmacologic therapy if my DEXA shows osteopenia?”  
- “Any medication changes that would help reduce fracture or fall risk?”  
- “What fall-prevention measures or physical therapy do you recommend?”

B. Cardiology referral — when it’s appropriate for someone in your situation
1. Factors that increase the need for cardiology input
- Symptoms: chest pain/angina, exertional shortness of breath, syncope/lightheadedness, palpitations or documented arrhythmia (esp. AF).  
- If you are on apixaban for atrial fibrillation and are not already co-managed by cardiology — many patients with AF are followed by cardiology for rhythm/stroke-risk decisions, anticoagulation management, and rate/rhythm control. If anticoagulation is for prior VTE, an anticoagulation clinic or PCP + hematology may be sufficient. Ask why apixaban was started.  
- Uncontrolled hypertension despite meds, abnormal ECG, prior heart attack, heart failure, or structural heart disease on echo.  
- Complex medication management (e.g., combining anticoagulants with other interacting drugs) or planning procedures that raise cardiac risk.

2. Cardiac risk evaluation options your PCP might use or refer for
- ASCVD risk calculators / lipid management and aggressive risk factor control for diabetes.  
- If intermediate risk, your PCP might order a coronary artery calcium (CAC) score to refine risk and guide statin therapy.  
- Stress testing or cardiology referral if you have symptoms suspicious for ischemia or reduced functional capacity.  
- If you have atrial fibrillation, cardiology can help with rhythm control strategies, ablation consideration, and long‑term anticoagulation decisions.

3. Questions to ask your PCP about cardiology referral
- “Why am I on apixaban? Do I need cardiology follow-up for this medication or my cardiac care?”  
- “Given my diabetes and age, do you think I should be referred to cardiology for risk stratification or to check my heart (ECG or echo)?”  
- “Do I need a baseline ECG, stress test, or CAC score given my risk factors?”  
- “Is my blood pressure and heart rate control adequate on current meds (metoprolol 25 mg)? Any adjustments needed?”  
- “Should I see cardiology routinely, or only if I develop symptoms?”

C. Practical next steps to prepare for the visit
- Clarify the items I asked above (A1C value, reason for apixaban, prior DEXA). I can draft an email to Dr. Wilson requesting specific tests if you’d like.  
- Bring or have available recent labs (A1C, lipids, BMP, urine albumin) and any prior DEXA reports.  
- Print or copy the short question list above to use at the visit.  
- If you fall into thresholds for DEXA or have symptoms of cardiac disease, ask for those referrals proactively at the visit.

D. Quick safety notes
- Because you take apixaban, if any procedures (like DEXA — which is noninvasive — or a lab requiring a blood draw) are planned, anticoagulation rarely needs to be interrupted for DEXA, but for invasive procedures you'll need to coordinate with whoever manages the apixaban. Make sure the PCP and any specialist know why you’re on apixaban.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message/email to Dr. James Wilson summarizing these concerns and requesting DEXA, FRAX, vitamin D lab, and advice on cardiology referral; or
- Create a one‑page checklist you can bring to the visit.

Which would you like me to do next?

