--- AGENT RESPONSE (FULL) ---
Length: 6610 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — below is a focused plan to use at your PCP visit (or to send to Dr. James Wilson). A few quick clarifying questions before I draft anything or recommend next steps:
- What is your sex/gender and (if female) menopausal status? Bone-screening guidance depends on this.
- Did you mean A1C 7.2 (or another value)? A1C of 1.2 is almost certainly a typo.
- Why are you on apixaban (AFib, prior VTE, other)? Any known heart diagnoses (CAD, prior MI, heart failure) or symptoms (chest pain, palpitations, syncope, worsening shortness of breath)?
- Any current osteoporosis/DEXA results, or prior fragility fractures?

Below is a concise, practical checklist and suggested questions you can use when you speak with Dr. Wilson — focused on osteopenia management and whether a cardiology referral (Colorado Cardiology) is appropriate.

1) Osteopenia / bone health — key points to discuss and tests to request
- Why this matters for you: Type 2 diabetes and retinopathy both increase fall/fracture risk (retinopathy → impaired vision → more falls; T2D is associated with higher fracture risk even if BMD is normal).
- Tests to ask for:
  - DEXA scan (bone density) if you haven’t had one recently — request T-scores for lumbar spine and hip.
  - FRAX 10-year fracture risk calculation (ask your PCP to calculate it and interpret it in the context of T2D).
  - Serum 25-OH vitamin D, serum calcium, basic metabolic panel (kidney function), TSH (if clinically indicated), and maybe CBC if clinically relevant.
- Lifestyle and supplementation:
  - Calcium intake target ~1,000–1,200 mg/day (diet + supplements as needed).
  - Vitamin D 800–2,000 IU/day as needed; treat deficiency if 25-OH D is low.
  - Weight-bearing and balance exercises, strength training, smoking cessation, limit heavy alcohol, home fall-risk assessment, vision care follow-up (important given retinopathy).
- When to consider medication:
  - Typical thresholds: treat if DEXA T-score ≤ −2.5 (osteoporosis), OR T-score between −1.0 and −2.5 (osteopenia) with FRAX 10-year major osteoporotic fracture ≥20% or hip fracture ≥3%.
  - Note: FRAX can underestimate risk in people with T2D — discuss treating earlier even with osteopenia because diabetes raises fracture risk.
  - First-line drugs: oral bisphosphonates (alendronate) or IV zoledronic acid; alternatives include denosumab or other agents depending on kidney function, tolerability, and bleeding/medication interactions.
- Safety/monitoring:
  - Check renal function before bisphosphonates/denosumab.
  - Discuss dental health (rare osteonecrosis of the jaw risk with antiresorptives) and dental clearance if starting therapy.
  - Fall-prevention plan given retinopathy (vision referral if overdue).

Suggested questions to ask Dr. Wilson about bone care:
- “Can we order a DEXA scan and FRAX assessment today given my diabetes and history of retinopathy?”
- “If my FRAX or DEXA suggests osteopenia, at what point would you recommend starting medication given my diabetes?”
- “Please check vitamin D and calcium and advise on supplementation; are there any secondary causes of bone loss we should evaluate?”
- “Can we review fall prevention strategies and ensure I’m up to date with eye care?”

2) Cardiology referral — when it’s recommended and what to consider
- Age 52 and diabetes increases cardiovascular risk, but age alone is not an automatic reason for cardiology referral.
- Reasons to refer to cardiology (Colorado Cardiology) include:
  - Known or new atrial fibrillation or other symptomatic arrhythmia (palpitations, syncope).
  - On apixaban for AF — consider cardiology for rhythm/rate management or if procedural planning is needed.
  - Known coronary artery disease, heart failure, unreconciled abnormal EKG, or new/worsening cardiac symptoms (chest pain, syncope, new exertional shortness of breath).
  - Difficult-to-control blood pressure despite multiple medications, or need for specialized cardiovascular risk stratification beyond primary care.
  - If you or your PCP want advanced testing (echocardiogram, stress testing, Holter monitoring) or second opinion about anticoagulation strategy or device therapy.
- Tests/drug management PCP can do first:
