- Any current cardiac symptoms (chest pain, shortness of breath with exertion, palpitations, syncope), uncontrolled blood pressure, or known coronary disease?
- Any chronic steroid use, smoking, heavy alcohol use (>3 drinks/day), or kidney disease?

Below I’ll outline key points you can take to your PCP (Dr. James Wilson), suggested questions to ask about osteopenia, and guidance on whether to request a cardiology referral (Colorado Cardiology).

Osteopenia / bone-health — what to consider and ask
- Screening/testing
  - Ask whether you need a DEXA scan now (if you don’t have a recent one). Typical guidance:
    - Women: DEXA routinely at age 65, earlier if risk factors. Men: screen at age 70 usually, earlier if risk factors.
    - Diabetes, history of fragility fracture, long-term steroids, low BMI, parental hip fracture, smoking, heavy alcohol, or other secondary causes → consider earlier DEXA.
  - Request FRAX risk assessment (10-year major osteoporotic and hip fracture risks) using your DEXA and clinical risk factors.
- Labs to consider before deciding treatment
  - 25-hydroxyvitamin D, basic metabolic panel (including creatinine/eGFR), serum calcium, TSH, and possibly PTH, CBC — to exclude secondary causes and ensure safe use of medications.
- Lifestyle and supplements
  - Weight-bearing and resistance exercise program, fall-prevention measures, tobacco cessation, limit alcohol.
  - Supplemental calcium (if dietary intake low) and vitamin D to maintain 25-OH D in recommended range (provider will advise target levels and dosing).
- When to treat medically (general guidance)
  - Treatment is usually recommended if DEXA T-score ≤ −2.5 (osteoporosis), or if osteopenia (T-score between −1.0 and −2.5) plus FRAX 10-year hip fracture risk ≥3% or major osteoporotic fracture risk ≥20%, or prior fragility fracture.
  - First-line therapy: oral bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate) or IV bisphosphonate (zoledronic acid) if oral is not tolerated or absorption issues.
  - Alternatives: denosumab, teriparatide/abaloparatide (for high risk/very low bone density), selective estrogen receptor modulators in specific patients.
- Special considerations with your medications/conditions
  - Apixaban (anticoagulant) does not usually rule out bone meds, but coordinate dental work before starting IV or long-term antiresorptives because of the (rare) risk of osteonecrosis of the jaw. Anticoagulation increases bleeding risk with dental extractions — plan with dentist.
  - GERD: oral bisphosphonates can worsen or cause esophagitis; if you have active GERD, the provider may prefer IV bisphosphonate or another agent.
  - Kidney function: if eGFR is low, some therapies are contraindicated or require caution — check renal function first.
  - Retinopathy/diabetes: diabetes is associated with higher fracture risk even with higher BMD; this supports earlier evaluation.
- Monitoring
  - If treated, repeat DEXA typically every 1–3 years depending on severity and response; monitor calcium, renal function, and dental health.

Suggested questions to ask Dr. Wilson about osteopenia
- “Given my diabetes and history of retinopathy, should we get a DEXA now? When was my last DEXA?”
- “Can you calculate my FRAX score and tell me whether I meet criteria for pharmacologic treatment?”
- “Please order 25-OH vitamin D, BMP (including creatinine), calcium, TSH (and PTH if you think it’s needed) before deciding on treatment.”
- “If I have GERD, are oral bisphosphonates appropriate, or should we consider IV zoledronic acid or another agent?”
- “What are the benefits and risks of starting a bisphosphonate or denosumab for me? Any special dental precautions because I take apixaban?”
- “If we treat, how often will you monitor my bone density and labs?”

Cardiology referral — when it’s reasonable and what to consider
Factors that favor referring to cardiology (Colorado Cardiology) now:
- Known atrial fibrillation that requires specialist input for rhythm/rate strategy or anticoagulation management — if your apixaban is for AF and you’re not already followed by cardiology, a referral is reasonable.
- New or ongoing cardiac symptoms: chest pain, exertional dyspnea, new palpitations, syncope, or presyncope.
- Known coronary artery disease, prior MI, heart failure, or reduced ejection fraction.
- Uncontrolled hypertension despite multiple meds, abnormal resting ECG, or abnormal cardiac testing.
- Complex medication management questions related to anticoagulation, device therapy, or invasive procedures.
- If you want a cardiologist to formally calculate ASCVD risk, consider stress testing, or optimize preventive cardiology beyond primary care.

When a cardiology referral is probably not needed immediately
- No cardiac symptoms, well-controlled blood pressure and lipids, no prior CAD/CHF/arrhythmia, and you’re asymptomatic — your PCP can usually manage risk reduction and coordinate cardiology referral if testing or symptoms develop.

Questions to ask your PCP to decide on referral
- “Why am I taking apixaban? Is it for AF or another indication? Do you recommend cardiology follow-up for anticoagulation management?”
- “Given my diabetes and age, what is my calculated ASCVD risk and does that justify seeing cardiology?”
- “Do I need an ECG or stress test before referral? Are there any abnormal vitals or findings that would prompt a cardiology consult now?”
- “If I’m asymptomatic, could we monitor and refer only if symptoms or abnormal testing appear?”

If you are referred, questions to ask a cardiologist
- “Does my cardiac history and risk profile (diabetes, age, medications) warrant additional testing or ongoing cardiology follow-up?”
- “If my apixaban is for AF, should we consider rhythm control options, monitoring (e.g., implantable loop recorder), or ablation?”
- “Any additional therapies or lifestyle changes you recommend to reduce my CV risk given my diabetes and retinopathy?”
- “Do I need an echocardiogram, stress test, coronary calcium score, or ambulatory rhythm monitoring?”

Other annual-physical items to bring up (useful to coordinate with bone/cardiac concerns)
- Confirm/update A1C (and discuss target range), fasting lipid panel, BMP, liver tests, urine microalbumin/creatinine.
- Resting ECG if there is any history of arrhythmia, palpitations, or if PCP recommends.
- Ask about vaccine status (influenza, COVID, pneumonia, shingles), cancer screening as appropriate.

Practical next steps I can help with
- Draft an email or message to Dr. James Wilson asking for:
  - Clarification of your A1C and confirmation of apixaban indication.
  - Ordering a DEXA and the labs listed (25-OH vitamin D, BMP, calcium, TSH).
  - A FRAX assessment and opinion on whether you need treatment for osteopenia.
  - His recommendation on whether you should have a cardiology referral now (and if so, referral to Colorado Cardiology).
- Help you prepare a one-page checklist to bring to your appointment with the above questions.

