Osteopenia / bone health — what to ask and expect
- Ask whether you need a DEXA scan (bone density). If you haven’t had one since menopause (if applicable) or in the past 2–5 years, you should get it. If you already have osteopenia on a prior DEXA, ask about repeat interval (often 1–2 years if close to treatment threshold or 2–4 years otherwise).
- Ask the clinician to calculate your FRAX score (10-year risk of hip and major osteoporotic fracture) and explain whether it meets treatment thresholds:
  - Typical treatment thresholds used in the U.S.: hip fracture risk ≥3% or major osteoporotic fracture risk ≥20% → consider pharmacologic therapy.
- Labs to consider before deciding treatment: serum 25-OH vitamin D, calcium, creatinine/eGFR, TSH (if indicated), and possibly serum protein electrophoresis or PTH if secondary causes suspected.
- Lifestyle and supplements: confirm target calcium intake (usually total 1000–1200 mg/day from diet + supplements if needed) and vitamin D dosing (commonly 800–2000 IU daily depending on level). Ask about weight-bearing exercise and fall-prevention strategies (vision, home safety, review of balance/neuropathy).
- Medication choices and special considerations:
  - Oral bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate) are common first-line treatments. BUT they can worsen GERD/esophagitis and require upright posture for 30 minutes after the pill. If you have active GERD or esophagitis (or difficulty swallowing), discuss alternatives.
  - IV bisphosphonate (zoledronic acid) or denosumab (Prolia) are effective alternatives if oral bisphosphonates aren’t suitable. Note: denosumab requires ongoing dosing and cessation can cause rebound vertebral fracture risk — plan long-term management before starting/stopping.
