Quick clarifying questions
1. Can you confirm your current medication list and indications? You wrote apixaban 5 mg twice daily and metoprolol 25 mg — what is apixaban for (atrial fibrillation, DVT/PE, other)? Is metoprolol for blood pressure / arrhythmia / heart disease? You also listed GERD — what medicine do you take for that (PPI like omeprazole)?
2. You said “last A1C was 1.2” — can you check that value? (A1C is usually reported as a percent; 1.2% would be unusually low; did you mean 7.2% or 12%?)
3. Have you had a prior DEXA (bone density) scan? If so, when and what were the T-scores (spine/hip)?
4. Any history of fragility fractures or frequent falls? Any other medications (steroids, aromatase inhibitors, anticonvulsants) or conditions that affect bone?

A. Osteopenia — what to ask and expect
Questions to ask your PCP
- Do I need a DEXA scan now (or repeat one)? If I already have osteopenia, what monitoring interval do you recommend?
- What is my FRAX 10-year fracture risk (with and without bone density)? Do I meet criteria for starting medication?
- Which labs should we check to rule out secondary causes of bone loss? (See tests below.)
- Should I start calcium and vitamin D supplements and at what doses?
- If medication is recommended, which drug would you prefer (oral bisphosphonate, IV bisphosphonate, denosumab, etc.) and why?
- Any special considerations because I take apixaban (bleeding risk) or metoprolol (falls/orthostatic hypotension)?

Tests and measurements commonly used
- DEXA scan (lumbar spine and hip) — to confirm T-scores and track change.
- FRAX score (10-year risk of major osteoporotic/hip fracture) — helps decide treatment when T-score is osteopenic.
- Labs to evaluate secondary causes and safety before treatment:
  - 25-hydroxy vitamin D
  - Basic metabolic panel (calcium, creatinine) — to check renal function (important for bisphosphonates)
  - Serum calcium and phosphorus
  - Parathyroid hormone (PTH) if abnormal calcium
  - TSH (thyroid disease can affect bone)
  - Consider CBC, liver tests if clinically indicated
- Fall risk assessment (gait, balance, home hazards)

When treatment is usually recommended
- Osteoporosis (T-score ≤ -2.5) → treat.
- Osteopenia (T-score between -1.0 and -2.5) → consider treatment if FRAX 10-year hip fracture risk ≥3% or major osteoporotic fracture risk ≥20% or if other risk factors (prior fragility fracture, high-dose steroids, recurrent falls).
- Clinical judgement matters (age, rapid bone loss, comorbidities).

Non-drug measures (important for everyone)
- Adequate calcium intake (diet + supplements if needed): typically 1,000–1,200 mg/day total depending on age/gender.
- Vitamin D: commonly 800–2,000 IU daily depending on level — dose individualized to maintain 25(OH)D >20–30 ng/mL.
- Weight-bearing and resistance exercises; fall-prevention (home safety, review of meds that cause dizziness).
- Stop smoking and limit alcohol.

Medication considerations and safety
- First-line: oral bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate) or IV options (zoledronic acid) — check renal function (generally avoid oral bisphosphonates if eGFR <30).
- Alternatives: denosumab (subcutaneous) or selective estrogen receptor modulators in select patients; each has pros/cons to discuss.
- Anticoagulation (apixaban) does not prevent you from taking osteoporosis medications, but discuss fall risk — if you have recurrent falls that might lead to major bleeding, discuss strategies to reduce falls and coordinate care.
