I can help — first a few quick clarifying questions, then practical points and suggested questions to take to your PCP (and an optional draft email to Dr. Wilson if you’d like me to send it).

Quick clarifications
- A1C of 1.2 is almost certainly a typo (typical A1C values are around 4–14%). Can you confirm your last A1C value and the date it was measured?
- Are you male or female, and (if female) are you post‑menopausal? Menopausal status matters for bone risk and treatment decisions.
- Why are you taking apixaban (atrial fibrillation, prior clotting event, other)? Are you already followed by cardiology for this?
- You listed “GERD (metoprolol 25 mg)”. Metoprolol is a beta‑blocker (cardiac) not a GERD medication — is there a different medicine you take for GERD (omeprazole, pantoprazole, etc.)?

Osteopenia — what to think about before your visit
Why diabetes and retinopathy matter
- Type 2 diabetes increases fracture risk independent of bone density; diabetic retinopathy increases fall risk because of vision problems.
- Those facts make a lower threshold reasonable for more aggressive bone-protection strategies.

Key items to confirm / ask your PCP
- Have you had a DEXA scan? If not, ask for a DEXA (bone mineral density) and request the T-scores at the femoral neck and lumbar spine.
- If you have DEXA osteopenia (T-score between −1.0 and −2.5), ask them to calculate a FRAX 10‑year fracture risk (or do it together). Note: FRAX may under‑estimate risk in people with diabetes, so discuss that with your clinician.
- Ask whether your FRAX 10‑year probabilities meet thresholds that typically trigger medication (common thresholds: ≥3% hip fracture risk or ≥20% major osteoporotic fracture risk) OR whether other factors (prior fragility fracture, very low T-score, ongoing steroid use, recurrent falls, secondary causes) would prompt treatment.

Tests and labs often useful when evaluating bone health
- Serum 25‑OH vitamin D
- Serum calcium and creatinine (or a basic metabolic panel) to check kidney function and calcium
- TSH (thyroid disease affects bone)
- If abnormal calcium or other concerns: PTH, albumin, phosphate, and potentially markers of bone turnover
- Consider vertebral fracture assessment or lateral spine x‑ray if height loss or back pain

Lifestyle and non-drug measures
- Vitamin D supplementation if low; typical dosing guided by level and PCP.
- Calcium intake: prioritize diet; supplements if intake is low (discuss with PCP).
- Weight‑bearing and resistance exercise, balance training (especially given retinopathy/fall risk).
- Fall prevention: vision care (timely retinal follow-up), home safety, review of medications that increase dizziness/falls, consider PT for gait/balance if falls or instability.

When to consider medications
- Indications commonly include: osteoporosis (T-score ≤ −2.5), fragility fracture, or osteopenia plus high FRAX risk or other high-risk features.
- First-line agents: bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate) are typical; alternatives include denosumab, SERMs, or teriparatide in select patients.
- If started on antiresorptive therapy, discuss duration (often 3–5 years then reassess) and any dental/orthopedic precautions (dental evaluation if long-term bisphosphonate is considered).

Cardiology referral — how to decide
Points that favor cardiology referral
- New or worsening cardiac symptoms: chest pain, new/exertional shortness of breath, syncope, new palpitations.
- Known atrial fibrillation or other arrhythmia (apixaban suggests anticoagulation for AF or prior VTE) — if AF is the reason for apixaban, many patients are co‑managed with cardiology (but some stable patients are managed by PCP).
- Known structural heart disease, reduced ejection fraction, recent hospitalization for cardiac issues, or if additional cardiac testing is being considered (stress test, echocardiogram interpretation, ablation, device therapy).

Items to bring up with your PCP (or to ask whether they’ll refer)
- Reason for apixaban and whether you have ongoing cardiology follow-up (if AF: when was last cardiology visit and last ECG/echo?)
- Chest symptoms or exercise tolerance changes since last visit
- Blood pressure and lipid control — ask whether current levels warrant cardiology input
- If you want cardiology input simply because of age + diabetes + anticoagulation, ask if PCP recommends cardiology review or whether routine monitoring with ECG and lipid control is sufficient

Specific tests/assessments you might request
- Resting ECG (if not recently done)
- Lipid panel and review of statin therapy (diabetes is a strong indication for lipid management)
- Echocardiogram if there’s a history of structural disease, new symptoms, or decreased exercise tolerance
- If AF: ask about last INR/renal function monitoring (for DOAC dosing), rhythm control vs rate control strategy review

Other annual‑physical items to include (given your diabetes + retinopathy)
- Updated A1C and BMP (kidney function)
- Lipids
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (kidney microvascular disease)
- Eye exam with ophthalmology (diabetic retinopathy follow-up)
- Foot exam and neuropathy screening
- Immunizations review (influenza, Tdap, shingles if appropriate, pneumococcal)
- Blood pressure check and review of home readings

Suggested questions you can say to Dr. Wilson at the visit
- “Can we review my last A1C and kidney function, and update labs today?”
- “Given my osteopenia history (and diabetes/retinopathy), should I have a DEXA now and have my 10‑year fracture risk calculated? If FRAX underestimates risk due to diabetes, how do you factor that in?”
- “If my FRAX or DEXA suggests higher risk, what bone treatment would you recommend and what are the pros/cons for me?”
- “Do I need cardiology follow‑up given I’m on apixaban and metoprolol? I want to be sure my rhythm, anticoagulation, and cardiac risk are optimally managed.”
- “Please check vitamin D, calcium, TSH, and BMP as part of the bone workup.”

