- How your conditions affect risk
  - Type 2 diabetes increases fracture risk independent of BMD. Retinopathy increases fall risk (vision impairment → falls → fractures). Age (52) is younger than many fracture guideline thresholds, but diabetes can push risk higher.  
  - GERD matters: oral bisphosphonates (alendronate/risedronate) can irritate the esophagus and may be poorly tolerated if you have significant GERD or esophagitis — IV zoledronic acid is an alternative.

- Thresholds for medication (general guidance — individualization needed)
  - Treat if DEXA T-score ≤ −2.5 (osteoporosis) or prior low-trauma hip/vertebral fracture.  
  - Consider pharmacologic therapy for osteopenia (T-score −1.0 to −2.5) if FRAX 10-year major osteoporotic fracture risk is high (many guidelines use ≥20% for major osteoporotic fracture or ≥3% for hip fracture as thresholds) or if there are additional risk factors (diabetes, prior fragility fracture, high fall risk).  
  - For men, criteria are similar but assessment is individualized — discuss male-specific thresholds with your PCP.

