Osteopenia / bone health — key items to discuss with your PCP
- Tests/assessments to request
  - DEXA scan (hip and lumbar spine) if you haven’t had one in the last 2 years (or sooner if you’ve had a fracture or rapid bone loss). If you have a prior DEXA, ask for the comparison and trend.  
  - FRAX 10-year fracture risk calculation (ask your PCP to run it with your DEXA results).  
  - Labs to screen for secondary causes: 25‑OH vitamin D, calcium, basic metabolic panel (kidney function), thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), fasting glucose/A1C (you already have diabetes), and if indicated PTH, CBC, liver tests, and SPEP (if clinical suspicion).  
  - Consider lateral spine imaging or vertebral fracture assessment (VFA) if there’s height loss or back pain — vertebral fractures can be silent.

- How your conditions affect risk
  - Type 2 diabetes increases fracture risk independent of BMD. Retinopathy increases fall risk (vision impairment → falls → fractures). Age (52) is younger than many fracture guideline thresholds, but diabetes can push risk higher.  
  - GERD matters: oral bisphosphonates (alendronate/risedronate) can irritate the esophagus and may be poorly tolerated if you have significant GERD or esophagitis — IV zoledronic acid is an alternative.

- Thresholds for medication (general guidance — individualization needed)
  - Treat if DEXA T-score ≤ −2.5 (osteoporosis) or prior low-trauma hip/vertebral fracture.  
  - Consider pharmacologic therapy for osteopenia (T-score −1.0 to −2.5) if FRAX 10-year major osteoporotic fracture risk is high (many guidelines use ≥20% for major osteoporotic fracture or ≥3% for hip fracture as thresholds) or if there are additional risk factors (diabetes, prior fragility fracture, high fall risk).  
  - For men, criteria are similar but assessment is individualized — discuss male-specific thresholds with your PCP.

- Medication considerations
  - First-line agents: oral bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate) or IV zoledronic acid. Teriparatide/abaloparatide reserved for very high-risk cases; denosumab is another option but requires consistent follow-up.  
  - Because of your GERD, discuss IV zoledronic acid or ensuring safe use of oral bisphosphonates (take standing with full glass of water, remain upright 30–60 minutes, ensure no active esophagitis).  
  - Dental evaluation: before starting antiresorptives (especially IV bisphosphonates or denosumab), consider dental exam because of small risk of osteonecrosis of the jaw with invasive dental procedures.  
  - Duration: typical initial course is 3–5 years for bisphosphonates then reassess BMD and fracture risk.

- Non‑drug measures to discuss
  - Vitamin D: check 25‑OH D level; target often ≥30 ng/mL (some guidelines use ≥20 ng/mL) — discuss supplementation dose based on your level.  
  - Calcium intake: dietary + supplements to reach ~1000–1200 mg/day depending on age/diet; discuss supplements only if diet inadequate.  
  - Weight-bearing and resistance exercise, smoking cessation, limiting alcohol, and fall-prevention strategies (home safety, vision optimization given retinopathy).  
  - Medication review for fall risk — beta blocker (metoprolol) can cause dizziness/hypotension in some; review orthostatic symptoms.

Cardiology referral — when it’s appropriate
- Reasons to consider a cardiology referral now
  - You have atrial fibrillation (apixaban suggests this possibility) — if so, cardiology follow-up often recommended for rhythm/rate management and stroke prevention planning. Ask if cardiology is already following you.  
  - Known coronary artery disease, prior MI, angina, heart failure, unexplained syncope, or new/worsening exertional chest pain or dyspnea.  
  - Abnormal ECG, abnormal stress test or echocardiogram, or difficulty controlling blood pressure despite multiple meds.  
  - If PCP thinks you need advanced cardiac risk stratification (stress testing, coronary CT angiography, echo) given your age + diabetes + anticoagulation.

- If you don’t have cardiac symptoms or known heart disease
  - Diabetes is a cardiovascular risk factor, but not everyone with diabetes needs routine cardiology referral; the PCP can often manage primary prevention (statin, BP control, aspirin if indicated, lifestyle) unless there are specific cardiac problems or symptoms.  
  - If you’re taking apixaban for AF or another cardiac indication, discuss whether cardiology is already coordinating anticoagulation care; if not, consider a one-time cardiology evaluation.

